\begin_layout Section
A consumer with two nests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A consumer buys three goods in quantities 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\geq0$
\end_inset

 at prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1},p_{2},p_{3}>0$
\end_inset

,
 and has income 
\begin_inset Formula $I>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Goods 2 and 3 belong together in a 
\series bold
nest
\series default
,
 while good 1 is on its own.
 Say good 1 is 
\series bold
food
\series default
,
 and goods 2 and 3 are 
\series bold
bus trips
\series default
 and 
\series bold
train trips
\series default
.
 Bus and train are both ways of travelling,
 so they are much more like each other than either of them is like food.
 That is all the nest says.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First the two travel goods are combined into a 
\series bold
travel composite
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{B}=\left[\beta x^{\rho_{B}}_{2}+(1-\beta)x^{\rho_{B}}_{3}\right]^{\frac{1}{\rho_{B}}},\label{eq:x_B}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and then food and travel are combined into utility,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3})=\left[\alpha x^{\rho_{A}}_{1}+(1-\alpha)x^{\rho_{A}}_{B}\right]^{\frac{1}{\rho_{A}}},\label{eq:u}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is the weight on food and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is the weight on the bus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two exponents are 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{A}=1-\frac{1}{\sigma_{A}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{B}=1-\frac{1}{\sigma_{B}}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{A}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{B}>0$
\end_inset

 say how easily the consumer can 
\series bold
substitute
\series default
 one good for the other:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{B}<1$
\end_inset

:
 bus and train are 
\series bold
complements
\series default
.
 The consumer wants both,
 and cannot get far by replacing one with the other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{B}>1$
\end_inset

:
 bus and train are 
\series bold
substitutes
\series default
.
 Either one will do,
 so the consumer mostly buys whichever is cheaper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the same for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{A}$
\end_inset

,
 between food and travel.
 We always use 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\neq1$
\end_inset

,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\neq0$
\end_inset

 and you never divide by zero in the exponent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The problem in nested budget shares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rather than in quantities,
 we write the problem in 
\series bold
budget shares
\series default
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}=\frac{p_{j}x_{j}}{I}$
\end_inset

,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}=\frac{s_{j}I}{p_{j}}$
\end_inset

.
 Utility is increasing in all three goods,
 so the consumer never throws money away and spends everything,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now describe the choice in the 
\series bold
same two steps as the nests
\series default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

:
 the share of income spent on 
\series bold
food
\series default
.
 That leaves the share 
\begin_inset Formula $1-s_{1}$
\end_inset

 for travel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $w\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

:
 the share of the 
\series bold
travel budget
\series default
 spent on the 
\series bold
bus
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The three budget shares then follow as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{1},\qquad s_{2}=\left(1-s_{1}\right)w,\qquad s_{3}=\left(1-s_{1}\right)\left(1-w\right),\label{eq:nested}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the quantities as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}=\frac{s_{j}I}{p_{j}}$
\end_inset

.
 The problem is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V=\max_{s_{1}\in\left[0,1\right],\,w\in\left[0,1\right]}\,u\left(\frac{s_{1}I}{p_{1}},\frac{\left(1-s_{1}\right)wI}{p_{2}},\frac{\left(1-s_{1}\right)\left(1-w\right)I}{p_{3}}\right).\label{eq:V}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We call 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(s_{1},w\right)$
\end_inset

 the 
\series bold
nested budget shares
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the reparametrization from section 6.4 of the optimization lecture.
 Whatever 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(s_{1},w\right)$
\end_inset

 you pick,
 the three shares in eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:nested"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 sum to one,
 so the budget constraint holds 
\emph on
exactly
\emph default
.
 The constraint set is therefore the box 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,1\right]\times\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 -- and a box is precisely what 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{L-BFGS-B}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 takes as its 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{bounds}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 No penalties and no constraint handling of any kind are needed anywhere in this project.
\end_layout

