\begin_layout Subsection
A two-dimensional grid search
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The idea is the one from the optimization lecture:
 lay a grid over everything the consumer can afford,
 compute utility in every point,
 and keep the best one.
 Nothing clever happens -- and that is the point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Make a grid of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 values of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 values of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

,
 both from 0 to 1,
 compute utility in every combination,
 and keep the best point.
 Implement it as 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{solve_grid()}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Report both 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(s_{1},w\right)$
\end_inset

 and the three budget shares it implies.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Hint
\bar default
:
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{np.meshgrid(s1_vec,w_vec,indexing='ij')}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 builds the grid,
