\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Hint
\bar default
:
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{optimize.minimize}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 calls the function you give it as 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{callback}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 once per iteration,
 with the current point as its only argument.
 That is the only way to see the path:
 the result object holds the point the solver stopped at,
 and nothing about how it got there.
 Note that an iteration is not a function evaluation -- the solver computes 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 several times per iteration,
 for the gradient and for the line search,
 and the callback fires only for the steps it actually takes.
 It is not called for the starting point either,
 so put 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{s0}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in the list yourself before you call the solver.
 Use 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{callback=lambda sk:
 path.append(sk.copy())}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{.copy()}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is defensive:
 if the solver reused one array and overwrote it,
 you would get the same point repeated.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
