\begin_layout Section
Lump-sum taxes and product taxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government needs revenue,
 and has two kinds of instrument:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
a 
\series bold
lump-sum tax
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

,
 which reduces income to 
\begin_inset Formula $I-T$
\end_inset

 and raises the revenue 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
product taxes
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{j}\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 which raise the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1+\tau_{j}\right)p_{j}$
\end_inset

 and raise the revenue 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{j}p_{j}x_{j}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Total tax revenue is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R=T+\sum^{3}_{j=1}\tau_{j}p_{j}x^{\star}_{j},\label{eq:R}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\star}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is what the consumer buys 
\series bold
given the taxes
\series default
.
 The consumer gets nothing back:
 the revenue leaves the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We compare the instruments in the simplest possible way.
 Two taxes that raise 
\series bold
the same revenue
\series default
 are compared by the 
\series bold
utility
\series default
 the consumer is left with.
 Higher is better.
\end_layout

